  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Homeland Security has obligated $69.1B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security executed 30,042 awards with an average award size of $2.3M, and obligations are up 143.7% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $19.7B in FY21 to $69.1B in FY26, with intermediate totals of $20.8B in FY22, $23.9B in FY23, $23.6B in FY24, and $28.3B in FY25. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest NAICS category at $30.3B. Ship Building and Repairing follows at $7.9B, then Security Guards and Patrol Services at $4.3B, and Process, Physical Distribution, and Logistics Consulting Services at $3.2B. Vendor concentration is notable: FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO is the top contractor at $13.4B, followed by BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED at $4.5B and DAVIE DEFENSE INC. at $3.5B. The Department of Homeland Security’s FY2026 to date profile shows heavy construction, shipbuilding, and security services spending, with a small set of vendors receiving very large obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has DHS contract spending changed year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security’s obligations are up 143.7% year over year. Fiscal obligations grew from $28.3B in FY25 to $69.1B in FY26. The Department of Homeland Security shows a multi-year increase from $19.7B in FY21 through $23.9B in FY23, with a dip to $23.6B in FY24 before the FY25 and FY26 increases. These figures reflect the fiscal-year-to-date snapshot for FY2026."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate DHS contracting?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction leads with $30.3B in obligations. Ship Building and Repairing is second at $7.9B, and Security Guards and Patrol Services is third at $4.3B. FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO is the largest vendor at $13.4B, followed by BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED at $4.5B and DAVIE DEFENSE INC. at $3.5B."
      }
